Source patch for the GNU build layer only -- applies to tools/makeprom.c.
Not a patch to sys/dropins.h (Sun's header, untouched); the fix is entirely
in makeprom.c's own two call sites.

Why: sys/dropins.h's ROUNDUP(n) macro casts through (unsigned int) before
rounding -- correct and invisible on the 32-bit ABI these tools were always
built for (a pointer and an unsigned int are both 4 bytes there), but a
silent 64-bit-pointer truncation on any modern LP64 host, x86_64 or
sparc64-linux-gnu alike. makeprom.c calls the generic, size-oriented
ROUNDUP() on a real pointer at exactly two sites; every other ROUNDUP()
call in the tree rounds an integer size, which this patch leaves untouched.

Root-caused 2026-09-19 by connecting gdb-multiarch to qemu-sparc64's own
gdbstub (`qemu-sparc64 -g <port> makeprom ...`, `target remote`, `catch
signal SIGSEGV`) rather than guessing from register dumps: the fault was
is_dropin() dereferencing a plainly truncated pointer (fits in 32 bits;
every real 64-bit heap address on the build host is far larger).

Fix: PTR_ROUNDUP(p), a build-layer macro in engine/compat.h that rounds
through uintptr_t instead of unsigned int, at the two call sites that round
a pointer.

--- a/tools/makeprom.c
+++ b/tools/makeprom.c
@@ -237,13 +237,13 @@
 			}
 		}
 		total = ((char *)obmdp) - start;
-		obmdp = (obmd_t *)ROUNDUP(obmdp);
+		obmdp = (obmd_t *)PTR_ROUNDUP(obmdp);
 	}
 	if ((verify) && (total < size)) {
 	    char *here;
 
 	    /* Scan forward looking for more headers.. */
-	    here = (char *)ROUNDUP((char *)obmdp);
+	    here = (char *)PTR_ROUNDUP((char *)obmdp);
 	    while (((here-start) < size) && (!is_dropin(here)))
 		here += 4;
 
